{"reply":"A strong companion is Plato’s *Meno*. In that dialogue, Socrates questions Meno’s confident definitions of virtue until Meno has to confront how little he can securely define; uncertainty becomes a possible beginning for inquiry. [Read *Meno* here.](https://classics.mit.edu/Plato/meno.html)

That sheds light on the Caterpillar’s opening in Chapter 5, “Advice from a Caterpillar”: “Who are _you?_” The question sounds simple, but it presses Alice’s unstable sense of self rather than asking for ordinary biographical information. Like Socrates, the Caterpillar makes an apparently settled answer difficult—but Carroll makes the encounter much less reassuring. Socratic questioning is presented as a route toward investigation, while the Caterpillar offers no clear guidance or trustworthy destination.

So I’d frame the connection this way: both scenes show confusion being produced by questions, but *Meno* leaves open the possibility that perplexity can help someone think more rigorously, whereas Alice’s scene asks whether a calm questioner is necessarily a helpful one. Does Alice seem to become more capable of inquiry here, or mainly less certain of who she is?","evidence_uses":[{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln0960-1016","source_location":"Chapter 5 — Advice from a Caterpillar, source lines 960-1016","exact_quote":"“Who are _you?_”","supported_claims":["That sheds light on the Caterpillar’s opening in Chapter 5, “Advice from a Caterpillar”: “Who are _you?_”","The question sounds simple, but it presses Alice’s unstable sense of self rather than asking for ordinary biographical information.","Like Socrates, the Caterpillar makes an apparently settled answer difficult—but Carroll makes the encounter much less reassuring.","Socratic questioning is presented as a route toward investigation, while the Caterpillar offers no clear guidance or trustworthy destination.","both scenes show confusion being produced by questions, but *Meno* leaves open the possibility that perplexity can help someone think more rigorously, whereas Alice’s scene asks whether a calm questioner is necessarily a helpful one."]},{"source_kind":"web","evidence_id":"https://classics.mit.edu/Plato/meno.html","exact_quote":null,"supported_claims":["A strong companion is Plato’s *Meno*.","In that dialogue, Socrates questions Meno’s confident definitions of virtue until Meno has to confront how little he can securely define; uncertainty becomes a possible beginning for inquiry.","Like Socrates, the Caterpillar makes an apparently settled answer difficult—but Carroll makes the encounter much less reassuring.","Socratic questioning is presented as a route toward investigation, while the Caterpillar offers no clear guidance or trustworthy destination.","both scenes show conf